  "original_html": "<section aria-labelledby=\"primary-heading\" class=\"flex w-full flex-1 flex-col h-full max-w-[1024px]\"><h1 id=\"primary-heading\" class=\"sr-only\">Home</h1><div><div class=\"max-w-[680px] m-auto px-1 flex flex-col gap-y-6\"><h1 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\">Exploring Arguments in Our Lives</h1><div class=\"border-b border-slate-200\"></div><div id=\"pm-1\" class=\"border-0 flex flex-col gap-y-4 ProseMirror\" data-context=\"doc\" data-input-id=\"pm-doc-html-c256bab6-57b9-4bf4-9181-dbb9a52d0b5b\" data-editable=\"false\" data-progress=\"\" contenteditable=\"false\" translate=\"yes\"><p>Framing Our Work</p><p><b>Routine Argument Writing helps us nurture community and civil discourse in our classrooms. </b>Regular low-stakes, high-impact writing invitations can support young writers as they build the skills needed to grapple with some of the complexities of advanced argument writing, such as debate, controversial issues, and researching opposing points of view. Equally important, these routine practices foster student agency when we invite them to engage in conversations about arguments in their lives.</p><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h___h_-finding-arguments-in-the-world\">🔎 Finding Arguments in the World</h3><p><b>Creating a culture of argument involves helping students notice and name the arguments already going on around them.</b> An easy place to begin is by noticing all the images that surround us every single day. By analyzing visual images, headlines, and even social media posts, students build interest and expand topic possibilities for their own arguments.</p><p><b>Consider the image below.</b> What argument is being made? How might this image spark conversations and curiosity to learn more?</p><div class=\"grid place-items-center\" contenteditable=\"false\"><div class=\"relative flex flex-row justify-between group w-full\" id=\"image-view-a9929652-108e-4fd3-93e4-d9f0e59f5b56\"><a data-type=\"image-ref\" class=\"self-center w-full\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/5d69d9e8-3cbc-4ae4-b538-be6402f78296/Discover-The-Forest-Ad-Campaign.jpg\" class=\"w-full\"/></a></div></div><p>Source: <a href=\"https://discovertheforest.org/\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Discover The Forest.org</span></a></p><p><b>Your turn! </b>Scroll through the slideshow below and consider how these images might spark conversations and curiosity to learn more. We invite you to share this slideshow with your students and respond to the images using these prompts:</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p><i>What argument is being made in each image?</i></p></li><li class=\"\"><p><i>What perspectives are represented?</i></p></li><li class=\"\"><p><i>What do you need to know in order to understand the argument?</i></p></li></ul><iframe src=\"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/e/2PACX-1vSld2LvBAIUknmYtgg0G_Rbzs29ynp_TPack48bidHw9J0xz2Iyrnvem9wPuh6aPv8wXqIO7G6ToJW-/embed?start=false&amp;loop=false&amp;delayms=3000\" height=\"475\" sandbox=\"allow-scripts allow-forms allow-same-origin allow-popups allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox\" title=\"Iframe for url: https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/e/2PACX-1vSld2LvBAIUknmYtgg0G_Rbzs29ynp_TPack48bidHw9J0xz2Iyrnvem9wPuh6aPv8wXqIO7G6ToJW-/embed?start=false&amp;loop=false&amp;delayms=3000\" style=\"width: 100%;\" contenteditable=\"false\"></iframe><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h___h_-try-and-share\">💬 Try and Share</h3><div class=\"grid place-items-center\" contenteditable=\"false\"><div class=\"relative flex flex-row justify-between group w-full\" id=\"image-view-a5c574c7-4313-41e5-84eb-6442bf53bb14\" style=\"width: 218.75px;\"><a data-type=\"image-ref\" data-width=\"35.267857142857146\" class=\"self-center w-full\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/76f13a11-ebca-4ac7-b59c-5281cf299faa/Discussion_icon_2022_copy_small.png\" class=\"w-full\"/></a></div></div><p><br class=\"ProseMirror-trailingBreak\"/></p><p>Add caption for image (optional)</p><p>Spend a day noticing arguments in your daily life: headlines, billboards, ads, social media posts, etc. <b>Gather some images that your students might be interested in talking about.</b> When curating images, consider:</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p>A range of topics and perspectives</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>Compelling images/headlines about relevant and current topics</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>Diversity and inclusivity <i>(for more on this see </i><a href=\"https://participate.community/collections/tips-for-choosing-diverse-and-inclusive-images-and-texts/993b3e86-d35d-48bf-94cc-bffcd3dbdff5\" class=\"underline\" target=\"\"><span><i>Further Exploration resources below</i></span></a><i>)</i></p></li></ul><p><b>Share your most compelling image in the </b><a href=\"https://participate.community/groups/24-25-secondary-smlc-source-based-argument/f05cccbb-83ae-40f9-bbb6-93a3f51ab73b/discussions/image-sparks/e145a08b-511e-4885-92a1-5fc3d91f5494\" class=\"underline\" target=\"\"><span><b>Image Sparks discussion</b></span></a>and <b>write a reflection</b> on how and why you selected the image.</p><p><i>Remember to &quot;follow the discussion&quot; to participate in the conversation</i></p><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h___h_-finding-arguments-in-our-day\">🔎 Finding Arguments in Our Day</h3><p><b>Once students begin to regularly notice arguments in the world around them, they can find even more ideas by looking at their own lives.</b> Every day, we have many experiences and make decisions that relate to larger conversations people are having in the world.</p><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h___h_daily-schedule\">Daily Schedule</h3><p><b>One place to discover argument topics is in our daily schedules.</b> Writers can use a <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/16WBalom-P0kmT3xbzWTT1_-Jjr6XCFb9CrAbsCx3egA/template/preview\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>clock organizer</span></a> or create one in their writing notebooks, then chart out how they spend 12 hours in a typical day. They can also simply list their schedule. Students then use their daily schedule to identify possible argument topics. <b>Notice the example below.</b> From this schedule, a teacher generated a topic list in the form of questions:</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p>Is breakfast <i>really</i> the most important meal of the day?</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>Best approaches to dog training?</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>Would a block schedule help mitigate learning loss?</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>What are the mental health benefits of taking breaks and going outside?</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>Are there benefits to doing nothing? Is it okay to do nothing?</p></li></ul><div class=\"grid place-items-center\" contenteditable=\"false\"><div class=\"relative flex flex-row justify-between group w-full\" id=\"image-view-0a28c14a-ed31-4904-a445-01669091695c\"><a data-type=\"image-ref\" class=\"self-center w-full\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/e5e66f8f-0230-41f0-b472-6c77775515e7/Schedule_Clock_Greg_Raney.jpg\" class=\"w-full\"/></a></div></div><p>Schedule by Greg Raney, South Coast Writing Project at UC Santa Barbara</p><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h___h_quicklists\">Quicklists</h3><p><b>Another way to find argument topics is by creating a quicklist.</b> We want to give student writers choices, but saying, “write about anything you want” without supporting them is often no choice at all. This strategy gives students a structure for generating a list of potential argument topics that they can return to again and again.</p><p><b>To create a quicklist,</b> have students number from 1 to 10 in their notebooks. Share each prompt (below) and have students quickly jot down their responses. Students return to the list and code it using an A for every topic that lends itself to an argument. These quicklist prompts can be adapted and changed throughout the year to discover more arguments.</p><div class=\"grid place-items-center\" contenteditable=\"false\"><div class=\"relative flex flex-row justify-between group w-full\" id=\"image-view-15dc231e-43f2-40d8-8fc5-dfbf6dfca4e6\"><a data-type=\"image-ref\" class=\"self-center w-full\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/f8b1dc39-97cc-460b-800d-9ce3b65fdb83/Quick_List_image_Ulysses_Cirilo.jpg\" class=\"w-full\"/></a></div></div><p>Quicklist by Ulysses Cirilo, fourth grade teacher in Chicago</p><p><br class=\"ProseMirror-trailingBreak\"/></p><p><b>Quicklist</b></p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p>1–2 List two things you did yesterday</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>3–4 Two things you like that others might not</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>5–6 Two conversations you have had recently (in-person or text/social media)</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>7–8 Two things you don’t like that others do</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>9–10 Two things you don’t think you could live without</p></li></ul><p><b>From here, writers have a ready-made list of argument topics.</b> Students can return to this list for daily short writing, to practice specific argument skills like finding evidence or writing claims, or for longer self-selected research projects.</p><p><br class=\"ProseMirror-trailingBreak\"/></p><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h___h_-try-and-share\">💬 Try and Share</h3><div class=\"grid place-items-center\" contenteditable=\"false\"><div class=\"relative flex flex-row justify-between group w-full\" id=\"image-view-dc2d1621-7150-456a-9922-d6434902e994\" style=\"width: 137.5px;\"><a data-type=\"image-ref\" data-width=\"22.023809523809522\" class=\"self-center w-full\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/05784e3e-8ff2-44b4-98b3-06199162292b/Discussion_icon_2022_copy_small.png\" class=\"w-full\"/></a></div></div><p><br class=\"ProseMirror-trailingBreak\"/></p><p>Add caption for image (optional)</p><p><b>Find some arguments in your own day!</b> Make your own daily schedule or quicklist, then <b>take a picture and post it in the </b><a href=\"https://participate.community/groups/24-25-secondary-smlc-source-based-argument/f05cccbb-83ae-40f9-bbb6-93a3f51ab73b/discussions/finding-arguments-in-our-day/e34bcee8-b7c1-43f8-b4c5-16d2d209336a\" class=\"underline\" target=\"\"><span><b>Finding Arguments in Our Day discussion</b></span></a><b>.</b></p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p>What potential argument topic(s) did you discover?</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>How might you use this approach with students in your classroom?</p></li></ul><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h___h_\"><br class=\"ProseMirror-trailingBreak\"/></h3><p><br class=\"ProseMirror-trailingBreak\"/></p><p><br class=\"ProseMirror-trailingBreak\"/></p></div><div id=\"doc-capture-source\" class=\"hidden\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div><ul class=\"flex flex-col absolute border-2 rounded-md bg-white outline-0 z-20 text-slate-500\" id=\"slash-menu\" tabindex=\"0\" style=\"display: none;\"><li class=\"group flex gap-2 cursor-pointer p-2 items-center text-slate-600 focus:bg-slate-100 focus:text-slate-900 focus-visible:outline-none hidden\" tabindex=\"-1\" id=\"slash-menu-h2\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" fill=\"none\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"currentColor\" class=\"size-6 stroke-slate-400 group-focus:stroke-slate-500\"><path stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" d=\"M21.75 19.5H16.5v-1.609a2.25 2.25 0 0 1 1.244-2.012l2.89-1.445c.651-.326 1.116-.955 1.116-1.683 0-.498-.04-.987-.118-1.463-.135-.825-.835-1.422-1.668-1.489a15.202 15.202 0 0 0-3.464.12M2.243 4.492v7.5m0 0v7.502m0-7.501h10.5m0-7.5v7.5m0 0v7.501\"></path></svg><span class=\"text-sm\">Heading</span></li><li class=\"group flex gap-